+## Subtopics
+
+### Regular 2D Packing
+A simpler packing strategy used when dataset sparsity is high. It safeguards memory performance by detecting potential overhead early and bypassing the greedy pipeline [S1][S2, unofficial].
+
+### Greedy Partitioning
+The process of dividing the volume into sub-volumes based on seed voxels and profit metrics (new seed voxels vs. empty voxels) [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Memory Efficiency:** Achieves an average 30.9% reduction in memory usage compared to the default 3D texture mapping baseline [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Comparison to Linked-List Sparse Storage:** Unlike methods such as Kauker et al. [Kau16] which store only non-empty voxels, this method avoids pointer bloat. At higher dataset densities (e.g., 34.6%), linked-list approaches inflate memory footprint by ~43% and degrade performance to ~1.9 fps, whereas this hybrid approach maintains efficiency [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** The method is designed for real-time rendering in medical imaging contexts. While specific FPS for this hybrid method is not explicitly quantified in the provided notes, it aims to overcome the performance bottlenecks of linked-list methods at high densities [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Effective memory optimization for both dense and sparse datasets (via the threshold mechanism); avoids pointer overhead; compatible with standard 3D texture mapping pipelines.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires CPU pre-processing time; the sparsity threshold value is not defined, making implementation tuning dependent on empirical testing.
+*   **Memory Efficiency:** Achieves an average 30.9% reduction in memory usage compared to the default 3D texture mapping baseline [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Avoids the performance degradation seen in linked-list methods (which drop to 1.9 fps at 34.6% occupancy) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Hardware Constraints:** Requires uniform texture dimensions for all slices, necessitating the post-packing optimization step [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **LOD:** Uses standard mipmapping to create a discrete Level of Detail system, improving rendering speed and visual stability at the cost of slightly increased memory for lower-resolution textures [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Bypasses severe 3D texture padding waste of AMR structures and complex decoding overheads of out-of-core streaming [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Greedy approach is less effective on sparse datasets; requires CPU pre-processing time [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-*   **Origin:** Proposed by Wei-Ming Yang and Robert Sadleir from Dublin City University in a preprint dated July 2026 [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Context:** Volume rendering based on 3D texture mapping is widely used in CT/MRI medical imaging but is constrained by GPU memory bottlenecks. Previous greedy packing strategies effective in 2D texture mapping introduce memory inflation when adapted to native 3D texture mapping due to uniform dimension constraints [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Prior Work:** The approach builds upon CPU-GPU hybrid concepts (e.g., Yang and Gu [Yan05]) and addresses limitations in sparse storage methods like those by Kauker et al. [Kau16] and Shear-Warp methods [Lee22] [S1, unofficial].
+This method was proposed by Wei-Ming Yang and Robert Sadleir from Dublin City University [S1][S2, unofficial]. It addresses limitations in existing volume rendering techniques, such as Shear-Warp (software-based, requires multiple volume copies) and empty-space skipping with linked lists (pointer bloat) [S1][S2, unofficial]. The approach builds on prior work in 2D greedy packing (e.g., MAXRECTS-BSSF) and adapts it for 3D texture mapping via a hybrid CPU-GPU pipeline [S1][S2, unofficial]. It is primarily used in CT/MRI medical imaging volume rendering [S1][S2, unofficial].
 
